[SIZE=6][B]🌐 v0.4.5 — Watch Somebody Else's Session[/B][/SIZE]

v0.4.5 lets one person drive and another person watch — not a summary of it, the whole session, on every screen. Plus the fix a driver reported here last week, and two more found while chasing it. Website: [URL]https://proengineer.app[/URL]

[IMG]https://proengineer.app/screenshots/LAN.png[/IMG]

[HR][/HR]
[SIZE=5][B]🌐 Somebody Else's Session, On Your Screens[/B][/SIZE]

Two people, two machines, one network. One drives; the other presses one key and their own screens fill with the driving happening on the other machine — the dashboard, the traces, the map, the corner table and the engineer.

[LIST]
[*][B]Every screen, not a copy of four sentences.[/B] The whole reading travels thirty times a second, so the watching copy finds the laps, builds the traces, draws the map's line and runs its own engineer over your driving. A spectator can ask the same questions the driver can.
[*][B]In their units and their language.[/B] The advice is written on the machine reading it, not on yours. Your friend sees °F and English if that is what they use.
[*][B]Two keys.[/B] Open [B]LAN[/B] — the new tenth tab, on [B]0[/B]. [B]S[/B] shares, [B]W[/B] watches. The port, the rate and being findable are all chosen for you.
[*][B]Nobody reads an IP address out loud.[/B] The copies announce themselves and appear in each other's list, with the car and track beside the name. Put the cursor on somebody and press [B]Enter[/B].
[*][B]It works with the graphical version too[/B], in either direction — terminal watching the window, window watching the terminal. One protocol as of this release.
[*][B]Nothing leaves your network.[/B] There is no server in the middle, because there is no server. What gets announced is a name, a role and a port — nothing about your driving.
[/LIST]

[B]Watching a friend in another city?[/B] It needs no relay and no port forwarding: a mesh VPN (Tailscale or ZeroTier, both free for a handful of machines) puts both computers on one private network wherever they are. The watcher presses [B]W[/B], the driver types the watcher's mesh address, and that is it. About half a megabit a second, one way — less than a video call.

For a coach watching a driver, a friend on the sofa, or a spare laptop beside the rig running your session on its own screen.

[HR][/HR]
[SIZE=5][B]⚙️ New: Settings → SHARING[/B][/SIZE]

The half you set once and never look at again: readings a second, only send while I'm on track, how long a quiet link waits before saying so, whether you are findable, and whether a session is accepted from the whole network or only from this machine.

The LAN screen itself reports what is actually happening — who is sending, at what rate, how old the picture is, and how many readings never arrived. If the link is bad you can see that it is the link.

[HR][/HR]
[SIZE=5][B]🔧 Fixed[/B][/SIZE]

[LIST]
[*][B]The camber advice no longer argues with itself.[/B] Told to add negative camber on a car already at -3.5°, you would read the reason first — and it said "the outer shoulder is not being loaded through corners", which is the argument for doing the opposite. Too little negative camber means the tyre stands up and the [I]outer[/I] shoulder takes the load, and that is what it says now. The message also names the edge — "outer edge hotter" — instead of "contact patch inefficient" beside a number you had to work out for yourself. Reported by [B]MartialArts[/B] on the graphical version's thread; the engineer is shared, so both got the fix.
[*][B]"not a saved lap: missing field rpms".[/B] Choosing your own reference lap could refuse to open it. A field was added to the lap format and old files were never taught to survive it — so every lap saved before that release stopped loading, and the laps most worth keeping are the oldest ones. They open again, and the test that keeps it that way uses the exact file that failed.
[*][B]The demo now fills the Analysis tab.[/B] It only ever built one lap, and that tab is the half of the program that is about [I]comparing[/I] — one lap means nothing to overlay, nothing to subtract and a flat delta. Five laps now, so everything on that screen does what it does in a real session.
[*][B]An empty mapping is no longer mistaken for a running game.[/B] On Linux the bridge creates the shared-memory pages when it starts and leaves them when the game quits, so the app could read zeros and believe a car was there.
[*][B]engineer_probe prints the tread it judged[/B] — inner, middle, outer, the inner-minus-outer every camber verdict is made of, and the camber the wheel is actually running. When advice is questioned there was no way to ask the program what it [I]read[/I], only what it concluded. Now there is.
[/LIST]

[HR][/HR]
[SIZE=5][B]📋 Also[/B][/SIZE]

[LIST]
[*][B]lan_probe[/B] — a diagnostic that says which of the four parts of the network is at fault: your machine's address, the port, the group the copies find each other on, and whether anything is arriving. Run it before anybody is waiting on you.
[*]Sharing and listening each have their own switch, so turning one off keeps the address instead of making you type it again.
[*]The Russian translation covers the new screens.
[*][B]Both machines need v0.4.5 to watch each other.[/B] There is no half-working mode with the previous release — the screen says so by name rather than calling the traffic noise.
[/LIST]

[HR][/HR]
[SIZE=5][B]💾 How to Update[/B][/SIZE]

[B]Windows:[/B] launch your current version and let the Auto-Updater install v0.4.5. Restart Assetto Corsa afterwards so it picks up the new panel.

[B]Linux & Steam Deck:[/B] let the Auto-Updater install v0.4.5, then restart Assetto Corsa. [B]You do not need a new shm-bridge.exe this time[/B] — the telemetry frame did not change in this release, so every bridge from v0.4.0 onward keeps working. If you are coming from v0.3.x, press [B]on the launcher's overlay card to fetch one[/B].

The launcher may note that your bridge was built for an older release. That is a note, not a fault — it says so because it is worth knowing before something [I]is[/I] wrong. Fetch the matching one if you would rather have them in step.

[B]For LAN:[/B] update both machines. Open [B]LAN[/B] (key [B]0[/B]), one of you presses [B]S[/B] and the other [B]W[/B], and pick each other out of the list.

Prefer a clean install? The archive contains the Windows and Linux builds, shm-bridge.exe, and a loose copy of the overlay.

Not sure whether the panel is working? Press [B]on the launcher for a full report[/B]: it names the application, the panel and the bridge, and tells you which of the three does not fit.

